WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.721
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.721 --> 00:00:06.831
trace a while loop's repeated body, use a new

00:00:06.831 --> 00:00:09.941
input value to make progress, and explain why

00:00:09.941 --> 00:00:12.843
the loop stops when a valid value arrives.

00:00:14.003 --> 00:00:16.613
Repeated checks let applications handle

00:00:16.613 --> 00:00:19.089
different numbers of attempts without

00:00:19.089 --> 00:00:22.235
duplicating source code, while a clear stopping

00:00:22.235 --> 00:00:25.113
rule keeps the program from processing more

00:00:25.113 --> 00:00:26.853
input than the task needs.

00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:31.218
A workshop assistant is recording a request for

00:00:31.218 --> 00:00:32.106
supply kits.

00:00:32.106 --> 00:00:35.438
A valid request is a positive whole number of

00:00:35.438 --> 00:00:38.548
kits; zero and negative quantities must be

00:00:38.548 --> 00:00:39.214
rejected.

00:00:39.214 --> 00:00:42.546
The assistant needs to report the first valid

00:00:42.546 --> 00:00:43.138
request.

00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:47.176
The input is fixed text containing integers and

00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:48.352
a positive value.

00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:51.467
Our program will print each rejection, report

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:54.650
the first accepted quantity, and stop reading.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.626
After a worked example and a request that's

00:00:57.626 --> 00:01:00.948
valid right away, you'll predict a new sequence.

00:01:01.382 --> 00:01:04.629
Let's open Main.java and build the quantity

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:05.082
check.

00:01:09.162 --> 00:01:12.329
Main is the class containing our program, and

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:15.425
its main method is the named operation where

00:01:15.425 --> 00:01:16.622
execution begins.

00:01:16.622 --> 00:01:19.718
We'll add our statements inside that method.

00:01:19.718 --> 00:01:23.026
The import makes Scanner's short name available

00:01:23.026 --> 00:01:25.137
for reading our supplied text.

00:01:36.556 --> 00:01:38.655
The starting structure is ready.

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:41.738
For the first worked case, the supplied entries

00:01:41.738 --> 00:01:43.772
are negative five and six kits.

00:01:43.772 --> 00:01:46.921
We'll create a Scanner to read them in order and

00:01:46.921 --> 00:01:49.741
store its first integer in quantity, so the

00:01:49.741 --> 00:01:51.906
condition has a request to check.

00:02:11.278 --> 00:02:13.817
That first read stores negative five.

00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.974
Scanner has moved past that token, but six has

00:02:16.974 --> 00:02:17.935
not been read.

00:02:17.935 --> 00:02:20.955
The condition checks the stored quantity; it

00:02:20.955 --> 00:02:23.632
doesn't choose another input by itself.

00:02:23.632 --> 00:02:26.857
Let's add a while body that runs only while the

00:02:26.857 --> 00:02:29.328
current request is zero or negative.

00:02:50.419 --> 00:02:54.039
The while keyword introduces a test before every

00:02:54.039 --> 00:02:55.849
possible body execution.

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:59.393
Parentheses enclose the Boolean comparison, and

00:02:59.393 --> 00:03:01.656
braces mark the repeated body.

00:03:01.656 --> 00:03:05.049
When the comparison is true, the body rejects

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:06.030
that request.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.669
One body execution is an iteration.

00:03:09.648 --> 00:03:12.546
The rejection message alone leaves quantity

00:03:12.546 --> 00:03:13.220
unchanged.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.984
Without another read, the loop would keep

00:03:15.984 --> 00:03:18.208
testing the same invalid request.

00:03:18.208 --> 00:03:21.443
We'll assign the next integer to quantity inside

00:03:21.443 --> 00:03:22.050
the body.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.083
That read makes progress by advancing through

00:03:25.083 --> 00:03:26.498
the supplied entries.

00:03:39.082 --> 00:03:42.164
The body now prepares the next condition check.

00:03:42.164 --> 00:03:45.049
After rejecting negative five, the read will

00:03:45.049 --> 00:03:47.999
store six, and execution returns to the while

00:03:47.999 --> 00:03:49.967
condition with that new value.

00:03:49.967 --> 00:03:53.114
It does not recreate Scanner or repeat the first

00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:53.901
declaration.

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:56.917
We still need to report the quantity that ends

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:57.507
the loop.

00:04:11.636 --> 00:04:14.193
The accepted print is outside the loop.

00:04:14.193 --> 00:04:17.078
When quantity is positive, the comparison is

00:04:17.078 --> 00:04:20.094
false, so Java skips the body and reaches that

00:04:20.094 --> 00:04:20.487
print.

00:04:20.487 --> 00:04:23.569
This is termination: the repetition ends, while

00:04:23.569 --> 00:04:25.798
the rest of the program continues.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.486
Let's click Run to check our worked case.

00:04:35.943 --> 00:04:39.291
The output shows one rejection and Accepted: 6.

00:04:39.291 --> 00:04:42.638
Negative five makes the first test true, so the

00:04:42.638 --> 00:04:44.846
body prints once and reads six.

00:04:44.846 --> 00:04:46.983
Six makes the next test false.

00:04:46.983 --> 00:04:50.402
There is one iteration but two condition checks,

00:04:50.402 --> 00:04:53.393
including the check that stops repetition.

00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:57.651
The stored quantity changes because the next

00:04:57.651 --> 00:04:59.278
read is assigned to it.

00:04:59.278 --> 00:05:02.603
Our supplied data includes a positive value, so

00:05:02.603 --> 00:05:05.432
this run reaches its stopping condition.

00:05:05.432 --> 00:05:08.262
Each required token must exist and be an

00:05:08.262 --> 00:05:11.657
integer; handling missing or nonnumeric input is

00:05:11.657 --> 00:05:12.789
a later problem.

00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:15.403
A while loop can also skip its body.

00:05:15.403 --> 00:05:18.335
Let's replace the supplied text with seven

00:05:18.335 --> 00:05:18.753
alone.

00:05:18.753 --> 00:05:22.034
Seven already meets the positive-quantity rule,

00:05:22.034 --> 00:05:24.616
so the first condition will be false.

00:05:24.616 --> 00:05:27.826
The program should accept that value without a

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:29.641
rejection or another read.

00:05:37.694 --> 00:05:39.950
Only the input text has changed.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.335
Running the complete program will create a fresh

00:05:43.335 --> 00:05:46.155
reader and perform the first read again.

00:05:46.155 --> 00:05:49.469
The same condition will decide whether the body

00:05:49.469 --> 00:05:50.174
is needed.

00:05:50.174 --> 00:05:52.219
Let's run this boundary case.

00:05:58.969 --> 00:06:00.757
Only Accepted: 7 appears.

00:06:00.757 --> 00:06:03.689
The first condition is false, giving zero

00:06:03.689 --> 00:06:06.193
iterations and one condition check.

00:06:06.193 --> 00:06:09.411
No second read happens, which matters because

00:06:09.411 --> 00:06:11.199
there is no second token.

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:14.561
A while loop tests before its body, even on the

00:06:14.561 --> 00:06:15.419
first visit.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:19.787
We have checked a rejected request followed by

00:06:19.787 --> 00:06:22.550
acceptance and a request that is valid

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:23.423
immediately.

00:06:23.423 --> 00:06:26.477
Now change only the supplied text to zero,

00:06:26.477 --> 00:06:28.222
negative two, and three.

00:06:28.222 --> 00:06:31.567
Keep the reading order and condition the same.

00:06:46.301 --> 00:06:48.335
The new sequence is in place.

00:06:48.335 --> 00:06:51.561
Before clicking Run, predict every output line

00:06:51.561 --> 00:06:53.666
and count the body executions.

00:06:53.666 --> 00:06:57.032
Trace the quantity used by each condition check,

00:06:57.032 --> 00:06:59.277
including the final false check.

00:06:59.277 --> 00:07:01.661
Also identify the last input read.

00:07:01.661 --> 00:07:03.976
Pause here if you want more time.

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:15.394
Let's run the changed program and compare its

00:07:15.394 --> 00:07:16.958
output with your trace.

00:07:24.401 --> 00:07:27.595
There are two rejection messages, then Accepted:

00:07:27.595 --> 00:07:27.729
3.

00:07:27.729 --> 00:07:29.525
The first read stores zero.

00:07:29.525 --> 00:07:32.520
One iteration rejects zero and reads negative

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.448
two; the next rejects negative two and reads

00:07:35.448 --> 00:07:35.848
three.

00:07:35.848 --> 00:07:38.909
The final test is false, so the accepted print

00:07:38.909 --> 00:07:39.242
runs.

00:07:39.242 --> 00:07:42.370
That's two iterations and three checks, with no

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:43.501
read after three.

00:07:44.404 --> 00:07:47.260
Our quantity check meets the assistant's rule.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.116
The while condition decides whether to repeat,

00:07:50.116 --> 00:07:52.785
each read prepares a new value for the next

00:07:52.785 --> 00:07:55.517
test, and the final false check leads to the

00:07:55.517 --> 00:07:56.510
accepted result.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.304
Keeping the starting read, repeated work, and

00:07:59.304 --> 00:08:02.097
stopping decision clear helps us explain both

00:08:02.097 --> 00:08:04.829
repeated rejection and immediate acceptance.

00:08:06.009 --> 00:08:09.175
For a different rule, suppose a request must be

00:08:09.175 --> 00:08:10.387
at least two kits.

00:08:10.387 --> 00:08:13.552
How would you change the invalid condition, and

00:08:13.552 --> 00:08:16.785
which two neighboring quantities would test that

00:08:16.785 --> 00:08:17.391
boundary?

00:08:17.391 --> 00:08:20.422
Explain why the read still belongs inside the

00:08:20.422 --> 00:08:20.759
body.
